Step-by-step analysis:

**Entity 1 — Vessel.** The schema is a vessel AIS stream, so the central entity is the ship itself. Every record carries mmsi (always populated, unique per vessel), so mmsi is the subject identifier with template vessel_{mmsi}. The ontology class is bsm:Vessel (a Vehicle designed to operate on or under water). Inherited properties I can use: conceptName, alternateName, typeName, sourceIdentifier, vesselCategoryCode, vesselMaximumSpeed, hasAffiliation, hasGeographicLocation, hasAlternateIdentifier, hasObservation (declared on Vehicle, inherited by Vessel), plus the unattached hasVelocity and hasOrientation whose descriptions say "of the Object".

**Entity 2 — Location and Entity 3 — AbsolutePoint.** lat and lon are present on every record, and the ontology routes position through hasGeographicLocation to a Location, then hasGeometry to a Geometry. AbsolutePoint carries latitudeDecimal/longitudeDecimal, so both linked classes are evidenced and get their own subject_mappings, keyed per record as location_{mmsi}_{timestamp} and point_{mmsi}_{timestamp}.

**Entity 4 — Velocity.** speed and course populate bsm:Velocity (speedMetersPerSecond, absoluteBearingAngle), linked via hasVelocity, keyed velocity_{mmsi}_{timestamp}. Note the qualifier trap: absoluteBearingAngle is defined as course over ground, which matches the course column, while heading belongs to Orientation, not Velocity. Also a unit caveat: speedMetersPerSecond is defined in m/s but AIS speed arrives in tenths of knots; templates cannot convert, so I map it as the only speed property with reduced confidence.

**Entity 5 — Orientation.** heading maps to headingAngle ("direction the longitudinal axis points"), linked via hasOrientation, keyed orientation_{mmsi}_{timestamp}. The heading/course pair is the classic near-synonym trap; the schema has both columns distinctly, so heading→headingAngle and course→absoluteBearingAngle.

**Entity 6 — Observation.** Each record is a timestamped position report; bsm:Observation (observationDateTime) is reachable from Vehicle via hasObservation, so timestamp gets a home as observation_{mmsi}_{timestamp}. Moderate confidence.

**Entity 7 — GeopoliticalAffiliation.** hasAffiliation leads from Object to Affiliation, and GeopoliticalAffiliation is explicitly "flag state of a vessel". flag holds two-letter codes (PL, LT) matching geopoliticalAffiliationCode. Keyed geopolitical_affiliation_{flag}. ship_country ("Poland") is the same fact as a name, not a code — no name property exists on the affiliation, so it stays unmapped.

**Entity 8 — AlternateIdentifier.** The schema shows genuinely distinct identifier schemes for one entity (mmsi, imo, ship_id), which is the structural evidence required for this scheme+value class. mmsi is already the subject; ship_id fits sourceIdentifier better ("primary key or row identifier from the originating dataset" — ship_id is MarineTraffic's internal key); imo becomes an AlternateIdentifier with constant standard code "IMO", keyed alternate_identifier_{imo}.

**Near-synonym decisions among type columns.** type_name ("Frigate") maps to typeName, whose description explicitly cites vessel-type strings broadcast by maritime navigation systems. ais_type_summary ("Military") maps to vesselCategoryCode because its values match the stated category list (cargo, tanker, fishing, passenger, military, special-craft); shiptype ("35", a raw numeric AIS code) and market ("NAVAL") are weaker claimants and stay unmapped. max_speed maps to vesselMaximumSpeed, but the property is defined as maximum *design* speed while the column is the maximum *observed* speed — the column is silent on the design qualifier, so I flag this as ambiguous with lowered confidence rather than assuming the match.

**Ports and voyage data.** Port exists in the ontology, but no object property leads from Vessel (or anywhere reachable) to a Port — partOf is for location hierarchy only — so all port/destination/eta/distance fields are unmapped rather than orphaned. Dimensions (length, width, grt, dwt, draught, l_fore, w_left), year_built, rot, status, dsrc, utc_seconds, avg_speed, ship_class, eta_calc, eta_updated, last_port_time have no matching properties and are unmapped.

**IRI consistency check.** Each IRI value's transformation is copied character-for-character from its target's subject_transformation: location_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, point_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, velocity_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, orientation_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, observation_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, geopolitical_affiliation_{flag}, alternate_identifier_{imo} — all verified identical. No empty transformation objects are emitted; all class/property URIs were re-checked verbatim against the ontology.

